{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_011CeDe28j8HZ2gQ9SnhAhGC","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01YXLZpQrQ4qaNZFgr4sqKgS","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"cd /home/patrickd/deepseek-v4-flash-0731-cuda\ngrep -n \"^import\\|^from\" tools/decode_fit_probe.py | head\npython3 - <<'PY'\np='tools/decode_fit_probe.py'; s=open(p).read()\nold = \"\"\"    # THE COMPLETION'S HASH IS THE BIT-EXACTNESS CHECK, AND IT IS FREE.\n    # Sampling here is seeded (`seed=1000+rep`) and the engine reseeds its mt19937_64 per request,\n    # so a run of this sweep is deterministic given the same weights and the same kernels. Two arms\n    # of a kernel A/B that produce the same 48 completion hashes have produced byte-identical token\n    # sequences at every context from 128 to 12,288 -- which is the invariant DECODE_LADDER.md asks\n    # every kernel change to prove, measured on the same records that carry the timings. Recording\n    # it costs one sha256 of ~1 KB per leg.\"\"\"\nnew = \"\"\"    # The completion's hash, recorded because it costs one sha256 of ~1 KB per leg.\n    #\n    # READ THIS BEFORE USING IT AS A BIT-EXACTNESS CHECK. An earlier version of this comment claimed\n    # that sampling here is seeded (`seed=1000+rep`), that the engine reseeds per request, and that\n    # therefore two arms producing the same hashes have proven byte-identical token sequences.\n    # **The first two are true and the conclusion is false, and it was measured false on 2026-08-20.**\n    # A full baseline sweep run twice from two independent server starts -- same binary, same env,\n    # same seeds, no kernel change whatsoever -- differed on **21 of its 52 legs**, and they were the\n    # same 21 legs on which a real kernel A/B differed (evidence/decode_loop/fit_1p0_determinism.txt).\n    #\n    # So a hash MISMATCH here means nothing on its own: it is the engine failing to reproduce itself\n    # at least as often as it is the kernel under test. A hash MATCH is still informative, and the\n    # matching legs are worth a lot -- on ladder 1.0 the legs at ctx >= 1536 matched across three\n    # independent runs and carried identical `tau`, which is what made the paired timing comparison\n    # possible. Use it that way: as a filter that identifies which legs are comparable, NOT as a\n    # verdict on the kernel. The verdict comes from memcmp of the kernel's own output buffer.\n    # See wiki/measurement-and-traps.md 12 and DECODE_LADDER.md hard invariant 1.\"\"\"\nassert old in s; s=s.replace(old,new); open(p,'w').write(s); print(\"ok\")\nPY\npython3 -c \"import ast;ast.parse(open('tools/decode_fit_probe.py').read());print('parses')\"\nrm -f file && echo \"removed stray 0-byte 'file'\"","description":"Correct the disproven determinism claim"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2051,"cache_read_input_tokens":190944,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2051},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"0d70d126-0070-4925-875e-05d6ca119884","uuid":"c7b1f805-edbb-4109-95f8-a73e5b91df52","timestamp":"2026-08-20T07:53:51.934Z","request_id":"req_011CeDe27ceemcQxR7P6mbYe"}
